WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:03.909 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  few things inspire our collective  imagination more than the fragmentary  artifacts of ancient civilizations ones  that once prospered and are now lost to  time looking at objects like these can  make you wonder what other great  civilizations could have risen and  fallen without a trace long ago  fragments of the past is a fantasy world  that echoes the lost artifacts and  fragmented tales of classical history  it's an imaginary bronze age epic a vast  world inspired by ancient mediterranean  myth in civilizations it's a realm of  sun and sea of gods and heroes of war  and fine art this project is the  creation of talented italian artist and  writer massimiliano hamatinon negro he's  released an art book on the epic setting  that much of this video is based on and  i've included a link to it in the  description alongside other ways you can  follow and support him in his book he  depicts imaginary artifacts from this  world pieces of ancient weapons and  pottery and fragments of poems alongside  fully realized images of what these  civilizations might have looked like at  their height there's a lot to cover but  i'll give you a condensed overview of  what's been revealed so far i've found  that exploring the lore of this world  can be a bit like acting as an  archaeologist or historian just of  cultures which never existed sort of  like speculative archaeology if you will  where one pieces together artifacts and  scattered literature so for this entry  into the archive we'll head out into  this fictional world shifting through  the fragments to see what epic  civilizations might emerge the world of  fragments of the past is set on the  massive continent of ascadoria the  imagined record suggests these kuzai  civilization was one of the most  influential in the region according to  legend the zuzai began as a nomadic  horde that raged in the steps beyond the  mountains but came to settle in  ascadoria and built a great empire it  seems the is koozai were people of  contrasts who were revered as both  fearsome warriors and great lovers of  poetry and music the artist imagines the  escuzai worshiped a mysterious and  mighty cosmic beast called the  double-crested panther depictions of  this deity appear in many's kuzai art  fragments and seem to symbolize a notion  we might call change when these koozai  went to war or when death from sickness  or injury loomed over a loved one they  prayed to the double-crested panther for  protection only in the most desperate  and life-changing occasions would they  ask the help of the panther and they  never did so more than three times a  year believing themselves to be divinely  protected the azkuza seemed at one point  to have conquered much of ascadoria  amassing a naval fleet which gave them  control over most waterways with sleek  eel-like boats these koozai upheld their  expanding empire which was composed of  various great cities in particular  betrice was a powerful city-state during  these days serving as both a wealthy  coastal port and a stronghold of his  kuzai military influence some of these  images reveal the splendor of beatrice  in its prime and the unique  architectural style of these koozai  during the period  reading through other fragments i  learned the second culture that  populated ascadoria were the atoy of the  coast a great civilization in their own  right the atoy were a developed and  architectually distinct group long  before the iscoozai arrived in ascadoria  although the izkuzai took over muchatoy  territory in time these kuzai were  transformed by the very culture they  were at war with though in many ways  invaders these kuzai found themselves  transformed by the civilization of the  atoy and began to adopt their cultural  practices like embalming the bodies of  the dead most significantly some  fictional artifacts suggest that the is  kuzai adopted the atoy pantheon of the  gods praying to them in situations that  didn't require the aid of the cosmic  panther the itoi gods therefore became  the most common on the continent the  author has created art of weathered  statues that depict some of the key  deities like farya the goddess of wild  beasts chirta the goddess of the  underground waters  god of knowledge and irigornio the  headless god of music and violence it's  unclear if any of these gods had any  true power outside of myth fragments of  the past is of course a fantasy story so  perhaps these gods could be real within  the context of the narrative in blurring  the distinction between myths and  history however the author highlights  how much of the past often remains a  mystery there are certainly other things  about this fictional world that imply  the existence of the supernatural  particularly when it comes to a toy  rituals in some passages there are  rumors of certain the toy who have  tapped into an unknown ancestral power  drawing strength from prophetic dreams  these individuals were revered as  oracles and conducted processions in the  honor of a forgotten deity the four-eyed  zagrea the cyclically born the eel  ancestor pretty cool if you ask me  there are legends that all the toy  people came from the sea and were exiled  from the water by zagria for an old  unknown crime it's unclear if there's  any truth to this creation story however  beyond the atoy and the zuzai i found  whispers throughout the text of other  civilizations like the agraran builders  of underground catacombs and the  cochleadoi the worshipers of the stars  but much of the history of ascadoria has  yet to be uncovered so instead the last  finding i'll touch on is this figure  huzachi perhaps a spirit perhaps a beast  or perhaps a little god the writings are  intentionally ambiguous but no matter  what huzachi is definitely very old with  depictions of the creature dating back  to the most ancient civilizations of  ascadoria this trickster is apparently  quite mischievous and ill-behaved and  summoned by saying their name three  times behavior aside i'm definitely a  fan of this strange little figure  there's plenty more to the lore of  fragments of the past and plenty more  the author plans on adding in the future  although i don't think everything will  ever be fully revealed as the imagine  gaps are part of what makes this  fictional history feel believable so if  you found the world of fragments of the  past intriguing you can check out the  links to the creator's art book and  social media below and as always thanks  for watching if you enjoyed this entry  please lend your support and like  subscribe and hit the notification icon  to stay up to date on all things curious  see you in the next video and may the  immortal god shield you from misfortune  you

